In this lesson, we'll look at what makes a relation a function, and how you can tell them apart on a graph. So, a relation is just any rule that links x-values to y-values, kind of like a matching list of pairs. A function is a special kind of relation where every x-value is paired with exactly one y-value. That means no input is allowed to give you two different outputs. Here's the handy trick: the vertical line test. If you imagine sliding a vertical line across the graph, it should never hit the curve more than once. If it does, that one x is producing two y-values, so it's not a function. For example, the square of x is a function, because each x gives you only one y. But if you flip it sideways, so x equals the square of y, that fails the test. The number four, for instance, comes from both two and negative two. One last idea: a function is called one-to-one when different x-values always give different y-values, and many-to-one when two different x-values can share the same y, like with the square of x. Now let's see it in action.